ure and, for\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:690:added. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:691:that declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:696:the history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:698:Both stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:700:is the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:701:a consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:712:retention is complete, and therefore that it excludes `gap`. One consequence is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:714:excludes `gap` cannot also exclude `full` unless its capacity is genuinely\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:744:- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:763:- **a retention or completeness predicate** — retention is declared, through\n    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n    67\tconsume no handle and produce one.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   116\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   117\t  and never callbacks.\n   118\t- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n   119\t\n   120\t## The IO Model\n   121\t\n   122\tThree models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the\n   123\tthird.\n   124\t\n   125\t**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\n   126\tthen returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as\n   127\tordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something\n   128\tmust wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\n   129\trequires a host that can park at all.\n   130\t\n   370\tdeclaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are\n   371\tschematic. A bare namespace var could reach that state only through namespace\n   372\tglobals, which is the hidden state this contract retires.\n   373\t\n   374\tThis table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an\n   375\tordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor\n   376\tstandardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns\n   377\t`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.\n   378\t\n   379\tCode that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions\n   380\tdirectly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global\n   381\tregistry and no namespace-load registration side effect.\n   382\t\n   383\t## Surfaces\n   384\t\n   385\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   386\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   387\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   388\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   389\t\n   390\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   391\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   392\tAn operation a handle does not\n   393\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   394\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   395\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   396\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   397\toperation exists.\n   398\t\n   399\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   400\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   401\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   402\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   403\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   404\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   405\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   406\t\n   407\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   408\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   409\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   410\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   411\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   412\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   413\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   414\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   415\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   416\t\n   417\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   418\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   419\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   420\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   421\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   422\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   423\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   424\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   425\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   426\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   427\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   428\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   429\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   430\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   431\t\n   432\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   433\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   434\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   435\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   436\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   437\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   438\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   439\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   440\tthe handle.\n   441\t\n   442\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   443\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   444\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   445\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   446\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a reported `gap` and never\n   447\tas `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   448\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   449\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   450\t\n   451\t### Where the asynchrony goes\n   452\t\n   453\tThe IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous\n   454\twhether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in\n   455\ta callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n   456\tnot pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's\n   457\tboundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\n   458\tarrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and\n   459\tevery reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\n   460\twhen it chooses.\n   461\t\n   462\tThis is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\n   463\tthat the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a\n   464\tretained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary\n   465\tcode to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing\n   620\t  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.\n   621\t\n   622\tThere is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are\n   623\tauthoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.\n   624\t\n   625\t## Concurrency\n   626\t\n   627\tEach logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer\n   628\tsurface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream\n   629\titself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see\n   630\tWriting). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\n   631\tin the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's\n   632\toutcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing\n   633\tfurther about concurrent operations.\n   634\t\n   635\tThis follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one\n   636\ttruth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\n   637\treader that observes\n   638\t`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\n   639\tcontradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global\n   640\tview exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\n   641\tanswered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\n   642\tposition in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\n   643\tthis handle, the answer is read where it is.\n   644\tWhat an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\n   645\ta merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,\n   646\tand interpretation happens above the stream.\n   647\t\n   648\t## Retention and Gaps\n   649\t\n   650\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   651\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   652\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   653\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   654\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   655\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   656\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   657\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   658\t\n   659\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   660\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   661\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   662\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   663\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   664\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   665\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a reader that falls behind is\n   666\ttold what it missed.\n   667\t\n   668\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   669\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   670\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   671\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   672\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   673\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   674\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   675\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   676\t\n   677\t### Complete history\n   678\t\n   679\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   680\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   681\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   682\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   683\t\n   684\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   685\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   686\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   687\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   688\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   689\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   690\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   691\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   692\t\n   693\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   694\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   695\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   696\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   697\t\n   698\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   699\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   700\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   701\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   702\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   703\t\n   704\t**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n   705\tsame kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n   706\ta window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n   707\thappened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n   708\tCapacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n   709\tand never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n   710\t\n   711\tA complete-history transport declares the reader and writer surfaces, that its\n   712\tretention is complete, and therefore that it excludes `gap`. One consequence is\n   713\tworth stating so that whoever builds one owes it up front: **a transport that\n   714\texcludes `gap` cannot also exclude `full` unless its capacity is genuinely\n   715\tunbounded.** Refusing and evicting are the two answers to the same condition,\n   716\tand a transport that has given up one owes the other.\n   717\t\n   718\t## Composition\n   719\t\n   720\tDaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\n   721\tthat transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its\n   722\town cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\n   723\toutside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the\n   724\tpipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.\n   725\t\n   726\tA forwarder between streams is a single step (not a callback loop) over the\n   727\ttransport-agnostic stream algebra. Because the stream never blocks, cadence is\n   728\towned by the composition-supplied driver that repeatedly calls the step,\n   729\tyielding execution as needed by the host runtime.\n   730\t\n   731\tFlow control (a reader pausing a sender) is an interpreter's concern. A pause\n   732\thas to be a lease and not a switch to avoid permanently stuck states. Those\n   733\tsemantics live in `dao.lease.md`, whose facts are datoms on a medium\n   734\t(`dao.space`). DaoStream provides the honest baseline: a reader that falls\n   735\tbehind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader decides what\n   736\tthat means.\n   737\t\n   738\t## Explicitly Absent\n   739\t\n   740\tAbsent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n   741\t\n   742\t- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n   743\t  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.\n   744\t- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\n   745\t  returns; operation results are authoritative.\n   746\t- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one\n   747\t  reader's progress every other reader's data loss. It is coherent only while\n   748\t  exactly one observer exists; a forwarder, an indexer, or a DHT replica makes\n   749\t  it theft. Where the capability went, so no later plan reinvents it: **taking\n   750\t  is a write.** Removing a tuple from an append-only log is not a mutation but\n   751\t  an assertion that it no longer holds, which `dao.space` already expresses as\n   752\t  a retraction datom — Linda's `in` is observe-then-retract, not a stream\n   753\t  operation. And the hard part of a take was never the removal but the\n   754\t  *exclusion* of competing takers, which is a lease over datoms\n   755\t  (`dao.lease.md`), not something a stream can promise. v1's `drain-one!`\n   756\t  appeared to solve exclusion only because it ran in one process against a\n   757\t  local ring buffer.\n   758\t- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\n   759\t  kept cursors cover repositioning.\n   760\t- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n   761\t- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n   762\t- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n   763\t- **a retention or completeness predicate** — retention is declared, through\n   764\t  the transport's nature and the creation specification. Asking a handle would\n   765\t  be the same defect as asking whether it is closed: the answer is\n   766\t  configuration the composition already holds, and reading it back at runtime\n   767\t  invites code that branches on what it should have been wired with.\n   768\t\n   769\t## The v2 namespace is transient\n   770\t\n   771\t`dao.stream` exists to protect a working system while its consumers move,\n   772\tnot to live forever. When the last consumer has migrated under its own plan\n   773\tand legacy `dao.stream` is deleted, **`dao.stream` is renamed to\n   774\t`dao.stream`** — decided, not left open. An undecided coexistence of both\n   775\tnamespaces is a defect of the migration, not a steady state.\n   776\t\n   777\tThis is recorded here because the plan that carried it,\n   778\t`dao.stream.implementation-plan.md`, was consumed when its phases\n   779\tcompleted; the decision it left open outlived it, and a transient plan is\n   780\tsafe to delete only once nothing in it is still owed.\n     1\t# DaoStream WebSocket Transport\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`. That document is the\n     4\tcontract; this one specifies the WebSocket transport's implementation of it.\n     5\tWhere the two disagree, the contract wins.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What the Descriptor Names\n     8\t\n     9\tA WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.\n    10\tThe stream exists on the serving host — created there, retained there,\n    11\tclosed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not any client is\n    12\tcurrently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one attachment:\n    13\tconnecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and neither\n    14\tevent creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes `attach!`\n    15\thonest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because\n    16\tthe stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire.\n    17\t\n    18\tThis framing generalizes: **serving is what makes any stream remotely\n    19\tattachable.** A ring buffer is host-local only because nothing speaks for\n    20\tit. A host that composes a WebSocket endpoint in front of one is serving\n    21\tthat ring buffer; the endpoint produces a descriptor carrying its address and\n    22\tthe ring buffer's unchanged logical-stream identity, and remote\n    23\tinterpreters can attach. The transport owns no stream — it serves streams\n    24\tthe host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors\n    25\tresolve against \"the streams a network endpoint serves because serving them\n    26\tis what it is.\"\n    27\t\n    28\tA server that wants per-conversation state does not bend attachment into\n    29\tcreation: it `create!`s a fresh stream for the conversation and offers that\n    30\tstream's descriptor.\n    31\t\n    32\t## Surfaces\n    33\t\n    34\tA WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable**, plus `descriptor` as every\n    35\thandle. It has no reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make\n    36\tthe reader surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence\n    37\tthat cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.\n    38\t\n    39\tBoth a client handle and a server-side accepted-connection handle answer\n    40\t`descriptor` with reachability for the endpoint through which they attached\n    41\tand with the served stream's transport-independent `:dao.stream/identity`.\n    42\tTwo endpoints may therefore produce different descriptors for the same served\n    43\tstream, while every such result carries the same identity.\n    44\t`:dao.stream/attachment` distinguishes connections; neither the descriptor nor\n    45\tthe logical-stream identity does.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe writer surface is on the connection's **ordered outbound path** — the\n    48\tsequence of values accepted for sending on this attachment — never on the\n    49\tserved logical stream. `:dao.stream/ok` places the value in the outbound path;\n    50\twhat becomes of it at the far end is reported there, not here (contract,\n    51\tWriting).\n    52\t\n    53\tOutcomes this transport produces, and why it excludes the rest:\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Produces                                       | Excluded, and why                                                                             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | —                                              | Absent: the transport owns no stream. Dynamic dispatch for this type returns `not-found`.     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, `transport-error`  | `transport-error` covers only failures decidable here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that fails, local resources exhausted. `not-found`, and the reachability failures that need a remote answer, are displaced to the deposit medium. |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |\n    60\t| `append!`    | `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error` | — (the contract's whole set)                                                      |\n    61\t| `close!`     | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |\n    62\t| `cursor`, `next` | —                                          | No reader surface, per above.                                                                 |\n    63\t\n    64\t## The Duplex Model\n    65\t\n    66\tA WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the\n    67\tdesign keeps them separate:\n    68\t\n    69\t- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and\n    70\t  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.\n    71\t- **Inbound**: wire framing and protocol validation first either produce a\n    72\t  valid transport event or perform protocol teardown. The host's adapter then\n    73\t  deposits every produced event — payloads and connection lifecycle events\n    74\t  alike, judging none semantically — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this\n    75\t  boundary. The deposit medium is an ordinary DaoStream stream the host\n    76\t  composed; this transport defines no separate event mechanism and no bus of\n    77\t  its own. (In the v2 migration slice that stream is a ring buffer, under a\n    78\t  time-boxed exception recorded in ADR 0003; the adapter cannot tell what it\n    79\t  deposits into, so the later swap is composition-only.) From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,\n    80\t  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that\n    81\t  stream.\n    82\t\n    83\tOn a client boundary, the traffic deposit destination is wired when the host\n    84\tcomposes the boundary, before `attach!`; it is not an argument chosen by the\n    85\tattaching interpreter. A serving endpoint instead receives the fixed\n    86\tacceptance-handoff slots and boundary control medium defined under Serving;\n    87\tthe acknowledgement supplies each accepted attachment's traffic destination.\n    88\tThese are host-composed media with different retention duties: a traffic\n    89\tmedium records replayable events and may report gaps, while a handoff slot\n    90\tholds one capability offer until that specific offer is acknowledged. The\n    91\tcomposition also hands each reader its medium and cursor. Every interpreter takes its own\n    92\tperspective on the same deposited truth: one indexes the traffic, another\n    93\tcopies it to a file, a third ignores it. How arrivals are interpreted is the\n    94\tinterpreter's business, not the transport's configuration.\n    95\t\n    96\t**Attachment identity.** This transport distinguishes its attachments, so its\n    97\t`attach!` success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` as the contract\n    98\trequires: a descriptor names the served stream and cannot tell two attachments\n    99\tto it apart, and a handle is a host-local object that cannot be compared\n   100\tagainst data that crossed a codec.\n   101\t\n   102\tEvery event deposited for that attachment carries the same value under\n   103\t`:ws/attachment`. The key differs because the scopes differ: result keywords\n   104\tare the contract's and live under `:dao.stream/…`, while a deposited event is\n   105\ta stream element, where transport-owned keys live under the transport's\n   106\tnamespace. Correlation is by the value carried, never by the key's name.\n   107\t\n   108\tWithout that identity a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence with no\n   109\tdemultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and unattributable.\n   110\t\n   111\t**Server-minted attachment identity.** Before offering a server-side\n   112\taccepted-connection handle to the serving composition, the transport mints\n   113\tits attachment identity under the contract's server-minted-handle rule. The\n   114\tvalue is unique among attachments the boundary can distinguish and is not\n   115\treused during that boundary's lifetime.\n   116\t\n   117\tThe acceptance-offer event carries the value under `:ws/attachment`, and\n   118\tevery later deposited event for the connection carries the same value. The\n   119\toffer, its acknowledgement, the handle, and all later events therefore\n   120\tdenote one attachment. Client-side `attach!` continues to return the\n   121\tcorresponding client-local identity under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are\n   124\tdistinguishable and both are attributable:\n   125\t\n   126\t```clojure\n   127\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   128\t :ws/event      :ws/payload  ; or :ws/accepted :ws/opened :ws/closed\n   129\t                             ;    :ws/ended :ws/error :ws/not-found\n   130\t                             ;    :ws/transport-error\n   131\t :ws/value      <decoded>    ; payload events only\n   132\t :ws/handle     {:dao.stream/handle <handle>\n   133\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer :closable}}} ; accepted offers only; host-local\n   134\t```\n   135\t\n   136\tThe event kinds divide into four groups. **Acceptance offer** —\n   137\t`:ws/accepted` is deposited once into a serving handoff slot after the\n   138\trequested stream resolves and a server-side attachment handle is\n   139\tconstructed. It carries that writer+closable handle and its declared surface\n   140\tunder `:ws/handle`.\n   141\t**Resolution** — `:ws/opened`, `:ws/not-found`, or `:ws/transport-error` says\n   142\thow a client attachment turned out; exactly one resolution event is deposited\n   143\tper attachment, and it is what a retry policy reads. **Lifecycle** —\n   144\t`:ws/closed` when the connection went away and `:ws/ended` when the served\n   145\tstream itself ended (see Ending a served stream). **Traffic** —\n   146\t`:ws/payload`, carrying `:ws/value`, and `:ws/error`. `:ws/error` is a diagnostic and is never terminal by itself. A survivable socket error may be followed by no lifecycle event; a protocol failure is followed by the terminal event produced when its required teardown completes.\n   147\t\n   148\tOnce an attachment identity exists, **every connection end deposits exactly\n   149\tone terminal lifecycle event at each endpoint**, whether the close was local,\n   150\tremote, or caused by failed deposit, protocol teardown, admission expiry, or\n   151\tendpoint stop. `:ws/ended` is reserved for a served-stream close; every other\n   152\tend is `:ws/closed`. For a code-4000 served-stream close, both the initiating\n   153\tserver endpoint and the observing client endpoint deposit `:ws/ended`. A\n   154\tresolution event, if any, is deposited first. Thus a\n   155\tdisclaimer is observed as `:ws/not-found` followed by `:ws/closed`, while a\n   156\tpre-resolution connection failure is `:ws/transport-error` followed by\n   157\t`:ws/closed`. Only the host close-completion callback deposits the terminal\n   158\tevent, guarded by attachment state so racing close/error callbacks cannot\n   159\tduplicate it.\n   160\t\n   161\tAn accepted offer is host-local and never crosses a serialization boundary.\n   162\tIt does not share the traffic medium's retention window; Serving defines its\n   163\tbounded handoff medium and acknowledgement.\n   164\t\n   165\t**The adapter is the sole host-socket subscriber, and the raw socket never\n   166\tescapes the handle.** On hosts with additive registration it uses\n   167\t`addEventListener` or `.on`; on Dart it owns the socket's single subscription.\n   168\tIn every case it fans transformed events into the composed deposit stream.\n   169\t“Additive” describes adding interpreters and cursors to that stream, never\n   170\tadding another listener to the raw socket.\n   171\t\n   172\tWrapping is not judging — the adapter never looks inside `:ws/value`.\n   173\tDeposited-event envelopes do not cross the wire: attachment correlation is\n   174\tadded locally because each side already knows which socket delivered a frame.\n   175\tWire frames have their own minimal envelope, specified under The Handshake and\n   176\tElements and Serialization, so protocol control cannot be confused with an\n   177\tapplication value.\n   178\t\n   179\t**Traffic granularity is composition data.** A client boundary may reuse one\n   180\tmedium across reconnects because it has one active attachment at a time. A\n   181\tserving endpoint uses one application-traffic medium per accepted attachment,\n   182\tsupplied with the acknowledgement; only endpoint/pre-accept control facts may\n   183\tshare its boundary control medium. This does not alter acceptance handoff:\n   184\tserver writer capabilities travel through bounded handoff slots, never through\n   185\tevictable traffic history.\n   186\t\n   187\t```clojure\n   188\t(def result (ws/attach! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws\n   189\t                         :ws/host \"example.org\"\n   190\t                         :ws/port 9090\n   191\t                         :ws/path \"/streams/telemetry\"}))\n   192\t\n   193\t(def sock (:dao.stream/handle result))\n   194\t(def me   (:dao.stream/attachment result))   ; keep it — nothing else identifies\n   195\t                                             ; this attachment on the medium\n   196\t\n   197\t;; write: (append! sock msg)\n   198\t;; read:  interpreters observe the medium the host boundary deposits into,\n   199\t;;        keeping events whose :ws/attachment is `me`\n   200\t```\n   201\t\n   202\tNo inbound-event inbox accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket\n   203\tinbox would be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed,\n   204\tsized by nobody. Before acceptance acknowledgement, the endpoint retains only\n   205\tthe bounded pending connection associated with each configured handoff slot.\n   206\tAfter acknowledgement, retention of traffic lives exclusively in the\n   207\thost-composed traffic medium, composed and sized by the host.\n   208\t\n   209\tThere are no callback hooks — no `on-open!` / `on-message!` / `on-close!`\n   210\tsurface of the kind the host's own socket API offers. Every socket event\n   211\tbecomes data on the deposit stream — payload and lifecycle (opened, closed by peer, error)\n   212\talike, distinguishable by shape. A boundary deposits everything; whether\n   213\tanyone reads it is not the boundary's concern.\n   214\t\n   215\t### Serving\n   216\t\n   217\tServing is the mirror of attaching, and the deposit model is the same on both\n   218\tends: each accepted connection's inbound messages are deposited — enveloped\n   219\twith that connection's attachment identity, exactly as on the client — onto a\n   220\tstream the host composition wired.\n   221\t\n   222\t**Acceptance is a bounded, acknowledged stream handoff.** At endpoint\n   223\tconstruction, the host composition supplies a fixed, non-empty collection of\n   224\thandoff slots. Each slot contains:\n   225\t\n   226\t- a host-local offer medium with evict-oldest retention and capacity one,\n   227\t  written by the transport and read by the composition; and\n   228\t- a host-local acknowledgement medium with evict-oldest retention and\n   229\t  capacity one, written by the composition and polled by the endpoint driver.\n   230\t\n   231\tThe composition mints the offer cursor it will retain and the acknowledgement\n   232\tcursor `endpoint-step` will retain for every slot before starting the listener.\n   233\tNo connection can therefore deposit an offer before its reader position exists.\n   234\t\n   235\tThe transport has at most one outstanding attachment in a slot. After\n   236\tresolving the requested stream, it assigns a free slot, mints the attachment\n   237\tidentity and handle, retains the connection in the endpoint's bounded\n   238\tpending-accept state, and deposits:\n   239\t\n   240\t```clojure\n   241\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   242\t :ws/event      :ws/accepted\n   243\t :ws/handle     {:dao.stream/handle <server-side-writer-handle>\n   244\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer :closable}}}\n   245\t```\n   246\t\n   247\tinto that slot's offer medium. It does not send the wire `:ws/accept` frame\n   248\tor enable value delivery yet.\n   249\t\n   250\tThe serving composition polls the known offer slots. After it reads an offer\n   251\tand retains the handle in its own session state, it appends:\n   252\t\n   253\t```clojure\n   254\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   255\t :ws/command    :ws/accept\n   256\t :ws/deposit    {:dao.stream/handle <writer-handle>\n   257\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer}}\n   258\t :ws/admission  {:retention :evict-oldest\n   259\t                 :capacity <positive-integer>\n   260\t                 :value-domain :host-values}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '640,722p;722,770p'\nrg -n 'reader.*(falls behind|miss|evict)|handle.*(miss|gap|complete)|must know|must.*gap|loss.*report|reports.*gap|silent' docs/design/dao.stream.md\nrg -n 'full|gap|unbounded|bounded|capacity|evict|refus|retention' docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '1,180p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '640,722p;722,770p'\nrg -n 'reader.*(falls behind|miss|evict)|handle.*(miss|gap|complete)|must know|must.*gap|loss.*report|reports.*gap|silent' docs/design/dao.stream.md\nrg -n 'full|gap|unbounded|bounded|capacity|evict|refus|retention' docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '1,180p'\"","aggregated_output":"   640\tview exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\n   641\tanswered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\n   642\tposition in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\n   643\tthis handle, the answer is read where it is.\n   644\tWhat an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\n   645\ta merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,\n   646\tand interpretation happens above the stream.\n   647\t\n   648\t## Retention and Gaps\n   649\t\n   650\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   651\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   652\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   653\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   654\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   655\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   656\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   657\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   658\t\n   659\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   660\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   661\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   662\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   663\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   664\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   665\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a reader that falls behind is\n   666\ttold what it missed.\n   667\t\n   668\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   669\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   670\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   671\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   672\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   673\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   674\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   675\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   676\t\n   677\t### Complete history\n   678\t\n   679\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   680\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   681\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   682\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   683\t\n   684\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   685\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   686\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   687\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   688\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   689\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   690\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   691\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   692\t\n   693\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   694\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   695\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   696\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   697\t\n   698\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   699\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   700\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   701\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   702\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   703\t\n   704\t**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n   705\tsame kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n   706\ta window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n   707\thappened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n   708\tCapacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n   709\tand never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n   710\t\n   711\tA complete-history transport declares the reader and writer surfaces, that its\n   712\tretention is complete, and therefore that it excludes `gap`. One consequence is\n   713\tworth stating so that whoever builds one owes it up front: **a transport that\n   714\texcludes `gap` cannot also exclude `full` unless its capacity is genuinely\n   715\tunbounded.** Refusing and evicting are the two answers to the same condition,\n   716\tand a transport that has given up one owes the other.\n   717\t\n   718\t## Composition\n   719\t\n   720\tDaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\n   721\tthat transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its\n   722\town cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\n   722\town cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\n   723\toutside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the\n   724\tpipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.\n   725\t\n   726\tA forwarder between streams is a single step (not a callback loop) over the\n   727\ttransport-agnostic stream algebra. Because the stream never blocks, cadence is\n   728\towned by the composition-supplied driver that repeatedly calls the step,\n   729\tyielding execution as needed by the host runtime.\n   730\t\n   731\tFlow control (a reader pausing a sender) is an interpreter's concern. A pause\n   732\thas to be a lease and not a switch to avoid permanently stuck states. Those\n   733\tsemantics live in `dao.lease.md`, whose facts are datoms on a medium\n   734\t(`dao.space`). DaoStream provides the honest baseline: a reader that falls\n   735\tbehind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader decides what\n   736\tthat means.\n   737\t\n   738\t## Explicitly Absent\n   739\t\n   740\tAbsent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n   741\t\n   742\t- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n   743\t  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.\n   744\t- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\n   745\t  returns; operation results are authoritative.\n   746\t- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one\n   747\t  reader's progress every other reader's data loss. It is coherent only while\n   748\t  exactly one observer exists; a forwarder, an indexer, or a DHT replica makes\n   749\t  it theft. Where the capability went, so no later plan reinvents it: **taking\n   750\t  is a write.** Removing a tuple from an append-only log is not a mutation but\n   751\t  an assertion that it no longer holds, which `dao.space` already expresses as\n   752\t  a retraction datom — Linda's `in` is observe-then-retract, not a stream\n   753\t  operation. And the hard part of a take was never the removal but the\n   754\t  *exclusion* of competing takers, which is a lease over datoms\n   755\t  (`dao.lease.md`), not something a stream can promise. v1's `drain-one!`\n   756\t  appeared to solve exclusion only because it ran in one process against a\n   757\t  local ring buffer.\n   758\t- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\n   759\t  kept cursors cover repositioning.\n   760\t- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n   761\t- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n   762\t- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n   763\t- **a retention or completeness predicate** — retention is declared, through\n   764\t  the transport's nature and the creation specification. Asking a handle would\n   765\t  be the same defect as asking whether it is closed: the answer is\n   766\t  configuration the composition already holds, and reading it back at runtime\n   767\t  invites code that branches on what it should have been wired with.\n   768\t\n   769\t## The v2 namespace is transient\n   770\t\n79:  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n319:| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n445:reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n508:  converts a silent loss into a reported `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards\n598:what *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n665:deliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a reader that falls behind is\n675:that must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n686:the transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n694:origin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n701:a consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n60:| `append!`    | `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error` | — (the contract's whole set)                                                      |\n79:  deposits into, so the later swap is composition-only.) From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,\n80:  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that\n88:These are host-composed media with different retention duties: a traffic\n89:medium records replayable events and may report gaps, while a handoff slot\n162:It does not share the traffic medium's retention window; Serving defines its\n163:bounded handoff medium and acknowledgement.\n184:server writer capabilities travel through bounded handoff slots, never through\n185:evictable traffic history.\n203:inbox would be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed,\n205:the bounded pending connection associated with each configured handoff slot.\n206:After acknowledgement, retention of traffic lives exclusively in the\n222:**Acceptance is a bounded, acknowledged stream handoff.** At endpoint\n226:- a host-local offer medium with evict-oldest retention and capacity one,\n228:- a host-local acknowledgement medium with evict-oldest retention and\n229:  capacity one, written by the composition and polled by the endpoint driver.\n237:identity and handle, retains the connection in the endpoint's bounded\n258: :ws/admission  {:retention :evict-oldest\n259:                 :capacity <positive-integer>\n291:An outstanding accepted offer cannot be evicted: it is the slot's sole\n293:acknowledgement. Reuse may later evict the acknowledged offer, but by then\n296:capability. Thus eviction of an unacknowledged acceptance event is\n299:The handoff remains bounded. When no slot is free, the transport admits no\n302:an outstanding offer nor grows an unbounded pending queue.\n376:cannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the\n381:in every slot declare host-local reference admission, evict-oldest retention,\n382:capacity one, ability to carry their respective envelope (including the live\n384:one-outstanding-offer discipline — not a different retention mode — is what\n390:retention pressure from every other attachment.\n392:The server boundary control medium also declares evict-oldest retention,\n393:capacity, `:portable-values`, and a lifetime longer than the endpoint. Its\n396:- The destination's assembly declaration contains both **retention** and\n397:  **value domain**. Retention is evict-oldest with a host-declared capacity.\n402:  `:dao.stream/full`; under\n403:  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one\n404:  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an\n405:  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the\n412:  codec admits. A destination that evicts oldest but answers\n414:  one; retention mode alone is not admission.\n415:- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused\n420:  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination\n424:  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the\n429:Sizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps\n433:rising gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own\n436:The accounting is then honest, though not complete: declared retention\n437:pressure surfaces as reader