{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '368,444p;638,660p'\nrg -n -i 'reader-visible|reader.*told|reader.*gap|must.*know|missed|silent.*loss|loss.*gap' docs/design -g 'dao.stream*.md'\"","aggregated_output":"   368\t\n   369\tThe close code is the fast path for the common case — a healthy server ending a\n   370\tstream deliberately. The handshake's disclaimer is what makes every other case\n   371\tresolve as soon as a server is reachable. Neither is sufficient alone.\n   372\t\n   373\t### Deposit Admission\n   374\t\n   375\tThe adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and\n   376\tcannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the\n   377\tdeposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced\n   378\twhere it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:\n   379\t\n   380\tAcceptance handoff media obey the same declared-admission rule: both media\n   381\tin every slot declare host-local reference admission, evict-oldest retention,\n   382\tcapacity one, ability to carry their respective envelope (including the live\n   383\thandle in an offer), and a lifetime longer than the endpoint. Their additional\n   384\tone-outstanding-offer discipline — not a different retention mode — is what\n   385\tprevents capability loss.\n   386\t\n   387\tThe acknowledgement's per-attachment traffic medium obeys the same rule and\n   388\tmust outlive that attachment. Supplying it in the acknowledgement makes its\n   389\tcursor-before-delivery ordering explicit and isolates one attachment's payload\n   390\tretention pressure from every other attachment.\n   391\t\n   392\tThe server boundary control medium also declares evict-oldest retention,\n   393\tcapacity, `:portable-values`, and a lifetime longer than the endpoint. Its\n   394\tcursor is minted before listener start, as are the handoff cursors.\n   395\t\n   396\t- The destination's assembly declaration contains both **retention** and\n   397\t  **value domain**. Retention is evict-oldest with a host-declared capacity.\n   398\t  The value domain is either `:host-values`, which may retain host references,\n   399\t  or `:portable-values`, which admits exactly the boundary codec's structural\n   400\t  domain. A WebSocket traffic medium may use either; a handoff offer medium\n   401\t  must use `:host-values` because it carries a live handle. It never answers\n   402\t  `:dao.stream/full`; under\n   403\t  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one\n   404\t  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an\n   405\t  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the\n   406\t  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed\n   407\t  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting\n   408\t  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,\n   409\t  like any storage failure.)\n   410\t- The destination must also be able to **carry every envelope this transport\n   411\t  deposits** — attachment identifiers, lifecycle events, and every value the\n   412\t  codec admits. A destination that evicts oldest but answers\n   413\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-value` on some envelope is as unwired as a reject-mode\n   414\t  one; retention mode alone is not admission.\n   415\t- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused\n   416\t  when the boundary is composed. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before\n   417\t  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.\n   418\t- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit\n   419\t  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is\n   420\t  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination\n   421\t  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;\n   422\t  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary\n   423\t  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.\n   424\t  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the\n   425\t  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and\n   426\t  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary\n   427\t  `attach!`.\n   428\t\n   429\tSizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps\n   430\tappear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Whether\n   431\tthat window can be resized after the fact is the destination transport's\n   432\tdeclared nature, not this transport's business. An adaptive policy — grow on\n   433\trising gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own\n   434\ttelemetry, not a transport behavior.\n   435\t\n   436\tThe accounting is then honest, though not complete: declared retention\n   437\tpressure surfaces as reader-visible `gap`s, and a dead destination tears the\n   438\tattachment down. But a deposit that fails is a deposit that did not land, and\n   439\tneither can the teardown event land in the destination that just refused it —\n   440\tso in that one case the local failure is observable only through the deposit\n   441\tresult the adapter holds and through subsequent handle operations, and the\n   442\tpeer observes it only if teardown reaches the wire.\n   443\t\n   444\t## Envelope\n   638\t  resumption is possible by design; what history a rejoining client receives\n   639\t  (and how it states where it left off) is not yet specified.\n   640\t- Backpressure protocols above the stream (a reader publishing its progress\n   641\t  as data is interpretation, outside both this transport and the contract).\n   642\t  Needing nothing from this transport does not mean needing no specification:\n   643\t  a pause vocabulary both ends must recognize is interoperable wire content,\n   644\t  and it requires a durable home before it is built. `dao.lease.md` is the\n   645\t  intended home for its semantics and does not yet claim them; the wire form\n   646\t  is owed by neither that document nor this one until it does. The v2\n   647\t  migration plan has deferred flow control out of its slice for the same\n   648\t  reason, so nothing is being built against an unsettled vocabulary.\n   649\t- Some host socket APIs expose no reliable outbound high-water signal after\n   650\t  establishment. On such a host `append!` may accept into the host's opaque\n   651\t  buffer and the manifest excludes transient `full` for that state; this\n   652\t  bounded-observability asymmetry is explicit rather than an invented signal.\n   653\t- Relationship to the RPC layers built over streams.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:508:  converts a silent loss into a reported `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:666:told what it missed.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:673:eviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:675:that must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:701:a consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:388:must outlive that attachment. Supplying it in the acknowledgement makes its\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:403:  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:429:Sizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:437:pressure surfaces as reader-visible `gap`s, and a dead destination tears the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.file.md:139:reader that receives `:daostream/gap` sets its cursor to `(tail-position fs)` and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.file.md:151:  must know. Rejected.\n","exit_code":0,"stat